# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is git-ignored — never commit real endpoints or credentials.
# In a deployed container these are injected by the orchestrator instead (env / secret).

# --- Connection (required) ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Full URI: bolt://, neo4j://, or neo4j+s:// for TLS (e.g. Aura).
NEO4J_HOST=bolt://localhost:7687
NEO4J_PASSWORD=
# Optional.
NEO4J_USER=neo4j
NEO4J_DATABASE=neo4j

# --- Server ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# view.sh binds 127.0.0.1; the container image defaults to BIND=0.0.0.0 PORT=8080.
BIND=127.0.0.1
PORT=8901

# How long a fetched graph is reused before the next request refetches. 0 disables caching.
# The UI's refresh button bypasses this via ?refresh=1 regardless.
# GRAPH_CACHE_TTL_SECS=3600

# --- What to fetch (all optional) ---------------------------------------------------------
# Restrict the fetch to a subset of the database. Empty (the default) means "the whole graph":
# every label, every relationship type. Set these to carve a large graph down to the part you
# actually want to look at — filtering happens in Cypher, so it also cuts the query cost.
# GRAPH_NODE_LABELS=Service,Host,Team
# GRAPH_REL_TYPES=DEPENDS_ON,OWNS

# --- Graph shaping (all optional) --------------------------------------------------------
# Property keys tried in order when choosing a node's display name; the first one a node
# actually has wins. Falls back to the first string property, then to "?".
# GRAPH_NAME_KEYS=name,title,displayName,id,hostname,canonical

# Labels that namespace a node rather than describe it. Dropped when choosing the display
# label; the first one a node carries becomes its `group`. Comma-separated, empty by default.
# GRAPH_WRAPPER_LABELS=Base,Legacy

# Property keys never sent to the browser. Add anything sensitive in your schema — the API is
# unauthenticated, so this list is the last line of defence on what leaves the server.
# GRAPH_SKIP_PROPS=embedding,vector,password,token

# Caps on a single fetch. The viewer renders the whole graph, so these bound both server
# memory and what the browser can realistically lay out. A truncated fetch logs a warning.
# GRAPH_MAX_NODES=30000
# GRAPH_MAX_RELS=60000
# GRAPH_MAX_PROP_CHARS=600

# --- Layout (all optional) ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The force layout runs HERE, not in the browser — natively and across every core, once per
# cache fill instead of on every page load in every tab. Coordinates ship with /api/graph and
# the client renders them settled, with no simulation of its own.
# Ticks. More is tighter and slower; quality plateaus well before 1000.
# GRAPH_LAYOUT_ITERATIONS=300
# Barnes-Hut opening angle. 0 is exact O(n^2); higher is faster and coarser.
# GRAPH_LAYOUT_THETA=0.9
# Edge length scale — larger spreads the graph out.
# GRAPH_LAYOUT_SCALE=1.0
# Pull toward the origin; the only thing stopping disconnected components drifting apart.
# GRAPH_LAYOUT_GRAVITY=0.02
# Seeds the starting positions. FIXED ON PURPOSE: a changing seed reshuffles the whole map on
# every refresh and destroys the spatial memory that makes a persistent view worth having.
# GRAPH_LAYOUT_SEED=1

# --- Semantic search (OPTIONAL — off by default) ------------------------------------------
# /api/search is fuzzy + field-weighted (name > label > properties) with no configuration at
# all. Setting SEARCH_EMBED_URL additionally blends in embedding similarity, so a query can
# find a node by MEANING rather than spelling. Unset, no request is ever made and nothing here
# is a dependency — which is how the published image ships.
# Any Ollama-compatible /api/embed endpoint.
# SEARCH_EMBED_URL=http://localhost:11434
# SEARCH_EMBED_MODEL=nomic-embed-text
# Nodes per request, and a ceiling on how many get embedded (highest-degree first). Embedding
# is the slow part of a refresh; the hubs carry most of the meaning.
# SEARCH_EMBED_BATCH=64
# SEARCH_EMBED_MAX_NODES=5000
# SEARCH_EMBED_TIMEOUT_SECS=120

# --- Performance (all optional) ----------------------------------------------------------
# Response compression. A clear win behind a reverse proxy, a pessimization over loopback
# (CPU on both ends to shorten a transfer that is already free). Default on; view.sh sets 0.
# GRAPH_COMPRESSION=1

# Serve a SYNTHETIC graph instead of querying Neo4j — for benchmarking and CI. When set to a
# non-zero value, no endpoint or credential is needed and Neo4j is never contacted. The
# GRAPH_MAX_* caps do not apply: the fixture is sized purely by these three values.
#   make bench BENCH_NODES=25000
# GRAPH_FIXTURE_NODES=0
# GRAPH_FIXTURE_EDGES=3
# GRAPH_FIXTURE_SEED=42

# --- Frontend (build-time) ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Title shown in the tab and the HUD heading.
# VITE_APP_TITLE=Graph Viewer

# Which view mode the app starts in: 2 (default) or 3. Users can switch at any time with the
# toggle in the top right; this only sets the starting point. 2D is the default deliberately —
# a dense graph in 3D occludes itself, and 3D reads best on the sparse subgraph a search leaves.
# VITE_DEFAULT_VIEW_MODE=2

# Asset base path. Defaults to './' (relative), which lets ONE build work at any mount point —
# served at '/', '/tools/graph/', anywhere — with no rebuild. Only set this if your proxy needs
# absolute asset URLs; it hardcodes the deployment path into the bundle.
# VITE_BASE_PATH=/tools/graph/
